public class Main {
    public static String f(String input_string) {
        // Check if the input string contains a database assignment
        if (input_string.contains("database") && input_string.contains("(") && input_string.contains(")") && input_string.contains("=")) {
            // Extract the value inside the parentheses
            int openParen = input_string.indexOf("(");
            int closeParen = input_string.indexOf(")");
            String value = input_string.substring(openParen + 1, closeParen);
            // Return the value with a conducive assignment
            return "database = " + value;
        } else {
            // Attempt to evaluate the input string as a math operation
            try {
                // Replace "*" with "Math.multiply" and add Math in front to handle multiplication correctly
                input_string = input_string.replace("*", "*");
                // Use JavaScript engine to evaluate the input string
                javax.script.ScriptEngineManager manager = new javax.script.ScriptEngineManager();
                javax.script.ScriptEngine engine = manager.getEngineByName("js");
                Object result = engine.eval(input_string);
                return result.toString();
            } catch (Exception e) {
                // If the input string cannot be evaluated as a math operation, return the original input string
                return input_string;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(f("ACM"));
        System.out.println(f("1+2*3"));
        System.out.println(f("database(value)"));
    }
}
```
Note: This code uses the Nashorn JavaScript engine which is included in the Oracle JDK, if you're using OpenJDK you'll need to add it as a separate dependency.

Also, the script engine may throw an exception or return `null` if the input string is not a valid math expression. In that case, the function will return `null` or throw an exception, so you may want to handle those cases as well.

